  "actualTargetProofs": {
    "content/ladr/ch03.js": {
      "sameTargetStatementAndProof": true,
      "actualRootTarget": {
        "statement": "Suppose $V$ is finite-dimensional and $T\\in\\mathcal L(V,W)$. Then $\\operatorname{range}T$ is finite-dimensional and\n$$\\dim V=\\dim\\operatorname{null}T+\\dim\\operatorname{range}T.$$",
        "idea": "$\\operatorname{null}T$ is a subspace of $V$ (3.13), so it has a basis $u_1,\\dots,u_m$, which extends to a basis $u_1,\\dots,u_m,v_1,\\dots,v_n$ of $V$ (2.32). Then $Tv_1,\\dots,Tv_n$ is a basis of $\\operatorname{range}T$: it spans because $Tu_k=0$, and it is independent because $\\sum c_jTv_j=0$ puts $\\sum c_jv_j$ in $\\operatorname{null}T=\\operatorname{span}(u_k)$, which the basis forbids unless all $c_j=0$. Counting, $\\dim V=m+n$."
      }
    },
    "content/pma/ch05.js": {
      "sameTargetStatementAndProof": true,
      "actualRootTarget": {
        "statement": "Let $f$ and $g$ be defined on $[a,b]$ and differentiable at a point $x\\in[a,b]$. Then $f+g$ and $fg$ are differentiable at $x$, and so is $f/g$ provided $g(x)\\neq0$; moreover\n(a) $(f+g)'(x)=f'(x)+g'(x)$;\n(b) $(fg)'(x)=f'(x)\\,g(x)+f(x)\\,g'(x)$;\n(c) $\\left(\\dfrac fg\\right)'(x)=\\dfrac{g(x)\\,f'(x)-g'(x)\\,f(x)}{g(x)^2}$.",
        "idea": "(a) follows from the sum rule for limits (4.4). For (b) split the increment of the product as $f(t)g(t)-f(x)g(x)=f(t)\\,[g(t)-g(x)]+g(x)\\,[f(t)-f(x)]$, divide by $t-x$, and use $f(t)\\to f(x)$, which is Theorem 5.2. For (c) put the increment of $f/g$ over the common denominator $g(t)g(x)$; by 5.2 again it tends to $g(x)^2\\neq0$."
      }
    }
  },
